                                                             cq-level 1..63, cpu-used 0..9
                                                             (libaom C oracle submodule: v3.14.1 / 03087864)
svt-c         ~/work/zen/zenav1-svt-c     dfbfe849c          SVT-AV1 upstream C, `git describe` =
                                                             v4.2.0-62-gdfbfe849c (62 upstream commits
                                                             past v4.2.0, all authored at Meta);
                                                             qp 0..63, preset 0..13
svt-rust      ~/work/zen/zenav1-svt       c391e762d          pure-Rust SVT-AV1 still-picture port,
                                                             targeting SVT-AV1 v4.2.0; qp 0..63, preset 0..13
zenrav1e      ~/work/zen/zenrav1e         4d1ed620           rav1e fork, `git describe` = v0.1.4-109-g4d1ed620;
                                                             quantizer 1..255, speed 0..10

NOTE on the svt pair: svt-rust ports SVT-AV1 v4.2.0; the C tree measured here is 62 upstream
commits past that tag. The two were nevertheless BYTE-IDENTICAL on the calibration cell at
presets 0..13 except presets 5 and 6 (see the .md) — so the version skew is not a material
confounder for the modes reported, but it is real and is stated rather than hidden.

## Tooling

decoder              aomdec, libaom 3.14.1 (Homebrew) — `aomdec --rawvideo`
cross-decoder check  dav1d 1.5.4 (Homebrew)
SSIMULACRA2          fast-ssim2 @ 3f1e957 (v0.8.2-13-g3f1e957), path dep
butteraugli          butteraugli @ e49085e (v0.9.3-32-ge49085e), path dep — max-norm and 3-norm
corpus               codec-corpus @ 28205bb (v1.1.0-4-g28205bb)

## Colour + input handling (identical for all four)

BT.709, LIMITED range, 8-bit, 4:2:0. xtool does RGB8 -> YUV444 -> 2x2 box-averaged chroma ONCE;
the resulting .yuv is the byte-identical input handed to all four encoders. Scoring converts BOTH
the reference .yuv and the decoded .yuv back to RGB with the SAME inverse (chroma replicated), so
the score isolates codec loss: the reference-vs-reference control reads SSIM2 = 100.000000,
BA_MAX = 0.0, BA_3N = 0.0.

Stream shape: all four emit exactly OBU_TD + 7-byte OBU_SEQUENCE_HEADER + OBU_FRAME, i.e. 14 bytes
of non-frame overhead each, so the reported BYTES are directly comparable.

## Timed region (per driver, see benchmarks/xbench/*/src/main.rs)

zenav1-aom  EncodeCell::port_encode(&bootstrap)          — the port's whole frame encode
svt-c       svt_av1_enc_send_picture + EOS + drain       — init/alloc/thread spawn excluded
svt-rust    EncodePipeline::encode_frame_420             — EncodePipeline::new excluded
zenrav1e    send_frame + flush + receive_packet          — new_context / Frame build excluded

Untimed on the zenav1-aom side ONLY: one C-libaom encode at the matched config, run purely to
supply the sequence header. This is structural, not a shortcut — the port never authors a
sequence header (Gate-2 scope note in CLAUDE.md); it parses one and emits only the OBU_FRAME.
Every coded frame byte measured and scored is the port's own.

## Exact commands

  scripts/xbench_build.sh
  python3 scripts/xbench.py prep
  python3 scripts/xbench.py stage1 --out <tsv> \
      --q '{"zenav1-aom":44,"svt-c":40,"svt-rust":40,"zenrav1e":187}'
  python3 scripts/xbench.py control --modes '<modes>' --n 9 --size 1024
  python3 scripts/xbench.py stage2 --out <tsv> --modes '<modes>'
  python3 scripts/xbench.py bdrate --tsv <tsv> --ref svt-c --metric ssim2
  python3 scripts/xbench.py stage3 --out <tsv> --modes '<modes>' --targets 50,60,70,80,90

## Stage-1 quality operating point

Encode time depends on the quantizer, so the four were put on a byte-matched operating point
rather than a nominally-equal knob value. Measured on the 1024x1024 photo cell:
  svt-c qp 40      -> 4990 B      svt-rust qp 40    -> 4990 B (byte-identical to svt-c)
  zenav1-aom cq 44 -> 5014 B      zenrav1e q 187    -> 5090 B
i.e. all four within +-2% of ~5 KB. That is the operating point every stage-1 number uses.

## Known caveat in the stage-1 raw TSV

drv-aom's BYTES column in stage1_2026-08-01.raw.tsv is the FRAME OBU PAYLOAD (14 bytes less than
the whole-stream figure the other three report). The driver was changed to report the whole
stream for stage 2/3; stage 1 does not use the byte column for anything but the operating-point
check above.

## Stage 2 / Stage 3 modes and corpus

modes                zenav1-aom cpu-used 6 | svt-c preset 1 | svt-rust preset 4 | zenrav1e speed 7
                     (each encoder's SLOWEST mode that clears 1 MP/s at the 1 MP photo cell —
                      i.e. the RD-best point of the qualifying set)
extra control arm    svt-c preset 4 ("svt-c@p4"): the same C encoder at the Rust port's mode
corpus               8 x CID22 validation 512x512 (square)  [class: photo]
                     2 x CLIC 2025 final-test, 1024x1024 centre square  [class: photo-hr]
                     3 x gb82-sc screenshots, 1024x1024 NATIVE centre crop (no resampling)
                       [class: screen]
q grid               14 points over each encoder's FULL native quantizer range
                       0..63 scales: 10,14,18,...,62   |  zenrav1e: 40,56,72,...,248
                     measured SSIMULACRA2 coverage: -64 .. +92
BD-rate              PCHIP (monotone cubic) over log10(bpp) vs metric on the overlapping
                     quality interval, 1000-point Simpson integration; per image, then median
stage-3 search       identical for all four (scripts/xbench.py::target_search): anchor seed in
                     normalized quantizer space, one offset probe, bracketed secant; band ±1.0
                     SSIMULACRA2; max 8 encodes

## Stage-2/3 timing caveat

The `enc_ms` column in the stage-2/3 TSVs is INFORMATIONAL ONLY: the svt-c@p4 control arm and
the zenav1-aom screen-tools A/B ran concurrently with parts of stage 2/3 on the same box. No
timing number quoted in xbench_2026-08-01.md comes from those columns — every one comes from
stage 1 or the control runs, which ran alone.
